\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Conditional shift in the dataset causes the relative performance gain of our CxVAE over the GVAE. We show performance on datasets generated with different values of the $\lambda $ hyper-parameter, controlling the amount of conditional shift. For low values of $\lambda $ the conditional density of student aptitude given a score changed very little from one group to another, and so CxVAE and GVAE perform equally well. However, as $\lambda $ increases, the GVAE fails to disentangle.}}{57}{figure.caption.23}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:xy}{{3.4}{57}{Conditional shift in the dataset causes the relative performance gain of our CxVAE over the GVAE. We show performance on datasets generated with different values of the $\lambda $ hyper-parameter, controlling the amount of conditional shift. For low values of $\lambda $ the conditional density of student aptitude given a score changed very little from one group to another, and so CxVAE and GVAE perform equally well. However, as $\lambda $ increases, the GVAE fails to disentangle}{figure.caption.23}{}}
